the right trigger is pulled past halfway, once until it is let go (under 0.3), and the legend's RT entry, which a tap
does the same as. steam.installed(): whether Steam's program is there (steam.exe in Steam's folder on Windows, steam on
PATH elsewhere), signed in once or not. platform.start_program(): a program started on its own outside the app's
bundle (outside_bundle(), moved from steam.py), for a game manager's emulator started without Steam.

Changelog: 30-10-2026 12:54:34

Fixed. On Windows, in an app built with PyInstaller, Steam starts outside the app's bundle (platform's outside_bundle(),
round every steam.exe command and steam:// address): the bootloader's DLL directory and the PyQt6 hook's PATH entry are the
app's _internal folder, and a program the app started inherited both, so Steam, whose folder has no VCRUNTIME140.dll,
ucrtbase.dll or OpenSSL of its own, loaded the app's copies before Windows' own (owner, 30-10-2026: Open Steam and a
game's Play in shadPS4 Manager never got Steam running, where Steam started by hand did). The DLL directory, PATH,
QT_PLUGIN_PATH and QML2_IMPORT_PATH are taken away while Steam starts and put back after; Linux and an app run from
the checkout as before.

Changelog: 30-10-2026 00:42:29

Fixed. On Windows, steam.open_url() starts a Steam that does not run as a plain steam.exe and hands it the steam://
address once it has signed in (hand_over(), signed_in(): HKCU\Software\Valve\Steam\ActiveProcess, ActiveUser), off
the caller's thread (owner, 30-10-2026: Play and Open Steam after a reboot showed "Updating Steam" and stopped:
steam.exe -- steam://open/bigpicture as Steam's first start never made its login window, where a plain steam.exe
